{"id":"u-388e7aa0-3030-4316-bae2-1f323f0d383f","src":"community","title":"Viking HLM workout - Heavy Light Medium Version","author":"Jacob  D.","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/qj43sl-viking-workout-full-body-v3-1","desc":"This is a powerbuilding program used to gain strength in the 3 main exercises: Bench, Deadlift and Squat AND combining accessory exercises like the overhead press and bend over row.\nIF you load this up you will get super strong and super jacked! \nThe program will also add supersets with focus on strengthening advanced bodyweight exercises like pull-ups, chin-ups and dips. \nIf you want to be seriously shredded you got to hit these bodyweight exercises! \n\nProgresssive overload:\nHeavy, Light, Medium (HLM) is a simple way to organize your training week so each lift gets a heavy, a medium, and a light session across three days. The heavy day pushes intensity, the light day keeps you moving and sharpens technique, and the medium day sits in between.\n\nThe HLM idea goes back to coaches like Bill Starr, Mark Rippetoe, and Andy Baker, who used it to keep intermediate lifters progressing without burning out. Rotating the intensity day to day is much like a flexible daily undulating (DUP) split, and by the final week the medium day gets pushed hard enough that the program starts to look like a Texas Method setup, with one heavy intensity day and one hard volume day.\n\nEnter your 1RM for each lift on the Input tab, pick your rounding, and the whole 12 weeks calculate for you. Every medium and light load is just an offset below that week’s heavy load, and everything climbs week to week.\n\nHow long does the HLM program last?\nThis template runs 12 weeks: \nSix weeks of volume in Phase 1. \nThree weeks in Phase 2 as the intensity climbs. \nTwo weeks of heavier low-rep work in Phase 3\nFinal peak phase or \"test\". \nAfter week 12 you retest your maxes and start over with the new numbers. You may also choose to deload after week 12.\n\nPauses:\nup to 3-4min of rest between sets. Rest can and should increase as needed in the final phases/weeks\nAim to have no or limited rest between supersets\n\n Deload:\n is not included in the program. Personally I only add deload when or if my body needs extra rest. \n\nConsistency:\nThis program is designed to maximize your chances to stay consistent. Cut to the bone with the exercises that give a good bank for your buck! With only 3 days/week. \nConsistency is where the real gains are.\nWord of advice: Sometimes the hardest part of the workout is just getting your butt off the couch and in the gym! \nSo remember, if you're limited on time, better a small workout than none at all. This is where the real gains are!\n\nCredit: This template was inspired by liftvaults.com editor Kyle Risley interpretation of the HLM method.","level":["Intermediate","Advanced"],"goals":["Muscle","Strength","Bodyweight Fitness","Women's"],"equipment":["Garage Gym"],"minutes":90,"joins":0,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-10-06T07:53:29.591Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Monday - Heavy Weight","Wednesday - Light Weight","Friday - Medium Weight"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"pct":30},{"r":8,"pct":50},{"r":5,"pct":70,"x":5}]},{"ss":[{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":12,"pct":30},{"r":8,"pct":50},{"r":5,"pct":70,"x":5}]},{"n":"Chin-Up (Weighted)","sets":[{"r":5,"pct":70,"x":5}]}]},{"n":"Deadlift (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"pct":30},{"r":5,"pct":60,"x":3}]},{"ss":[{"n":"Overhead Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"pct":30},{"r":5,"pct":60,"x":3}]},{"n":"Pull-Up (Weighted)","sets":[{"r":5,"pct":60,"x":3}]},{"n":"Dip (Weighted)","sets":[{"r":5,"pct":60,"x":3}]}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Squat (Paused)","sets":[{"r":8,"pct":30},{"r":5,"pct":55,"x":3}]},{"ss":[{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":12,"pct":30},{"r":5,"pct":55,"x":3}]},{"n":"Bent Over Row (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"pct":30},{"r":5,"pct":60,"x":4}]}]},{"ss":[{"n":"Overhead Press (Barbell)","sets"